He knows a lot about the science stuff, Professor Dave explains.

8
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:35,160
So far we've examined a number of extremely common techniques for the chemistry laboratory.

9
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:37,640
Today we are going to do something a little different.

10
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,280
We are going to talk about how batteries work.

11
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:45,320
A battery works by converting chemical energy into electrical energy, and this is covered

12
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:51,160
in a branch of chemistry we call electrochemistry, which we discussed in the lecture series.

13
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:56,120
In the case of a type of electrochemical cell called a galvanic cell, the production of

14
00:00:56,120 --> 00:01:03,480
electricity is achieved by a spontaneous oxidation-reduction reaction, or a redox reaction.

15
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:09,400
These reactions work by coupling oxidation and reduction, or a transfer of electrons from

16
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,320
one substance to another.

17
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:14,240
In an oxidation there is a loss of electrons.

18
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:17,040
In a reduction there is a gain of electrons.

19
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:23,440
Since the redox reaction involves a flow of electrons, it can do work to produce electricity,

20
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:27,900
just like how flowing water in a river can do work to push a water wheel.

21
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:31,600
This is because electricity is a flow of electrons.

22
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:36,720
To construct a galvanic cell in the laboratory, we need to set up the oxidation and reduction

23
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:40,400
reactions separately and connect them together.

24
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,400
These two sides are what we call half-cells.

25
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:48,520
The electrons are produced by oxidation on the left side, which is called the anode,

26
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:54,000
where neutral metal atoms donate electrons to become ions and join the solution.

27
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,600
On the right side, called the cathode, the reduction takes place as ions in the solution

28
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:03,360
gain electrons and deposit onto the electrode.

29
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:08,400
In a common example of a galvanic cell, we have zinc on the anode and copper on

30
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:09,880
the cathode.

31
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:15,600
The zinc metal loses two electrons to become zinc 2 plus and goes into solution.

32
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:20,200
The solution is usually one of zinc ions such as zinc sulfate.

33
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:25,760
On the cathode, we have copper metal and a solution of copper ions such as copper sulfate.

34
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:32,320
The copper ions gain two electrons to become neutral copper and deposit on the copper electrode.

35
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:36,120
We must connect these two cells by what we call a salt bridge.

36
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:42,120
A salt bridge is a salt solution that is used to transfer ions and reduce the accumulation

37
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:44,220
of charge on either side.

38
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:49,060
When the half cells are prepared and ready to go, they will be producing electricity.

39
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:53,720
All you have to do is connect the metal electrodes to a light bulb or simply a volt

40
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:57,040
meter to measure the voltage that's going through.

41
00:02:57,040 --> 00:03:00,260
We are going to build a cell like this today.

42
00:03:00,260 --> 00:03:03,840
To build a galvanic cell, we're going to start with the two half cells.

43
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:09,340
We're just going to use two beakers for that, and in the beakers we'll add the two solutions,

44
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:11,160
zinc sulfate and copper sulfate.

45
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:16,200
We're then going to place the metal electrodes into each solution like this.

46
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:20,640
The zinc metal goes into the zinc sulfate solution and the copper metal into the copper

47
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:21,960
sulfate solution.

48
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:25,840
We'll then connect the two solutions with a salt bridge.

49
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:29,820
A salt bridge can be a YouTube that's filled with a salt solution, but it can

50
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:33,820
also be a paper towel that has been dipped in the salt solution.

51
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:37,600
Today we're going to use potassium iodide for our salt bridge.

52
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:41,520
Let's cut up some filter paper and dip it into our solution.

53
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:44,680
This is what we will use to connect the two beakers.

54
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:49,020
Now that we've created our two cells and connected them by a salt bridge, let's add

55
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:52,260
a volt meter so that we can measure the voltage.

56
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:55,620
And look at that, we are already producing electricity.

57
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:59,620
The reactions in the two half cells are producing a flow of electrons that are being

58
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:01,760
picked up by our volt meter.

59
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:06,040
As you can see here, we have a reading of approximately one volt.

60
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:10,880
In the lecture series, we talked about how to calculate the theoretical voltage of a galvanic

61
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:11,880
cell.

62
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:16,240
It is the case that each of these half cell reactions has a standard potential that's

63
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:18,360
already known and tabulated.

64
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:22,680
The potential of the cell as a whole is derived by the standard reduction potential at the

65
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:27,840
cathode minus the standard reduction potential at the anode, and that is how you calculate

66
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:29,720
the total voltage.

67
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:34,780
Once calculated, we can compare our theoretical voltage to the experimental voltage.

68
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:39,480
For our experiment, as we can see, our experimental voltage is pretty close to the theoretical

69
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:41,880
voltage.

70
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:45,920
Another experiment we can do is to figure out how the concentrations of the solutions

71
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:48,620
in the half cells affect the voltage.

72
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:51,800
This can be calculated using the Nernst equation.

73
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:55,480
As you can see here, it is really about the ratio between the two.

74
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:59,880
So if we measure this when both solutions are at half the original concentration, the

75
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:01,560
voltage will stay the same.

76
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:07,240
However, if we change only one concentration and not the other, the voltage will change.

77
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:11,880
Once we are done with this particular galvanic cell, we can replace one solution and electrode

78
00:05:11,880 --> 00:05:15,940
with another solution and electrode, such as nickel in this case.

79
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:20,660
We can then measure the voltage between that half cell and that of copper or zinc.

80
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:24,880
So the more half cells you have, the more pairings you can make.

81
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:28,360
With three half cells you can make three different galvanic cells.

82
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:32,520
If you have four half cells, you can make six different cells, and so forth.

83
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:36,960
But you still measure the voltage the same way every time.

84
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:41,540
So that's how chemical reactions can produce electricity, and that's also how the battery

85
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:44,280
in your cell phone or your car works.

86
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:48,260
Of course the batteries in your devices will have more sophisticated reactions happening

87
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:53,440
at the anode and cathode, but they still utilize the same concept of a redox reaction

88
00:05:53,440 --> 00:06:09,800
in half cells, connected by a salt bridge and some way to harvest the electricity.
